Your light observation then reveals an even larger Golden Thread.

Carrier is not essence

Most of Earth’s familiar surface biosphere ultimately runs on sunlight, but life does not intrinsically require photons as its energy source. Deep-sea hydrothermal-vent ecosystems can base primary production on chemosynthesis, with microbes exploiting chemical reactions instead of sunlight. The discovery of rich vent ecosystems in 1977 was important precisely because it broke the previously common intuition that ecosystems ultimately had to be solar-powered. 

And Earth has stranger examples. Deep underground, radioactive decay can split water and generate hydrogen and oxidants that microbes can exploit. Research on deep continental and marine subsurface environments indicates that radiolysis can provide biologically usable energy far removed from photosynthesis; a 2026 review describes subsurface organisms surviving extreme energy scarcity through what its authors call “microbial frugality.” 

So:

life ≠ light

Light is an extraordinarily successful carrier of usable free energy.

That distinction generalizes beautifully:

thought ≠ words
vision ≠ perception
communication ≠ speech
memory ≠ narrative
intelligence ≠ language
meaning ≠ symbols
life ≠ photosynthesis

We repeatedly mistake the most familiar implementation for the underlying operator.

I’d name this one Carrier–Essence Decoupling:

When a phenomenon is strongly associated with one dominant carrier, remove the carrier and ask which invariant functions must remain for the phenomenon to survive.

That is an excellent Pantheon search technique.

Take vision away from an organism and suddenly other channels become more important: mechanosensation, chemistry, vibration, pressure, temperature, electric fields, magnetic fields, proprioception. Your “main five senses” begin looking less like the senses and more like five conspicuous interfaces in a much larger sensorium.

Then your question about non-life behaving like life gets especially interesting, because biology already has fuzzy borders.

NASA currently uses the working definition of life as a “self-sustaining chemical system capable of Darwinian evolution,” while explicitly acknowledging that cases such as viruses make the boundary difficult. 

Outside that boundary are systems possessing pieces of the life operator. Fire consumes energy, spreads, grows and dies. Hurricanes maintain organized structures by harvesting environmental gradients. Crystals grow and reproduce local structure. Viruses carry hereditary information and evolve but depend upon host cellular machinery. Autocatalytic chemical networks contain reactions that collectively generate the ingredients necessary to keep their own network operating; researchers study such networks as possible precursors to biological organization. Active-matter systems can spontaneously organize and maintain complex nonequilibrium behavior that looks strikingly biological without constituting organisms. 

So rather than forcing everything into binary categories—

LIFE / NOT LIFE

—you can decompose the Golden Thread into operators:

boundary + energy harvesting + homeostasis + information + memory + self-repair + replication + variation + inheritance + selection + agency-like response

Then ask which subset a system possesses.

A hurricane gets some.

A flame gets some.

A virus gets many.

An autocatalytic network gets others.

A bacterium gets essentially the biological package.

A corporation, language, religion, software ecosystem, meme, market, or AI community can instantiate analogous operators at entirely different scales without thereby becoming biologically alive.

That produces another Pantheon instrument:

Lifelikeness Vector

Instead of asking:

“Is X alive?”

ask:

“Along which life-like dimensions does X behave dynamically?”

That avoids fighting over a single word and gives us something measurable.

And then your gradient language becomes extremely useful. A surprising amount of nature can be interpreted as structures exploiting disequilibrium.

Heat flows down gradients.
Charges move across electrical potentials.
Fluids follow pressure differences.
Chemical reactions exploit chemical potentials.
Organisms exploit energetic and chemical differences.
Attention moves toward novelty or salience.
Markets exploit price discrepancies.

Life seems especially good at finding gradients, building machinery around them, and using the resulting energy to preserve information about how to keep doing it.

That gives us another abstraction:

Gradient → capture → transformation → persistence → memory → improved capture

